# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE CHECKSUM -- but only for the files that are
# actually byte-hashed. This file is deliberately narrow.
#
# TWO registries pin SHA-256 digests of file CONTENT:
#
#   config/canonical_sources.yml    26 CSVs   data-raw/** and three at data/
#   config/calibration_targets.yml   anchors  data/anchors/**
#
# Both are verified with digest::digest(file = ...), which hashes RAW BYTES.
# Git's autocrlf rewrites LF to CRLF on Windows checkout, so every one of those
# files hashes differently there:
#
#   sensitivity_departure_rate  recorded d76c5a7fd544  Windows actual dbdff7d01307
#
# That surfaced as `R CMD check windows-latest` failing with "SHA-256 drift"
# while Linux and macOS passed -- a checksum gate reporting data corruption when
# the data was fine and only the CHECKOUT differed. Left alone it would train a
# reader to dismiss drift reports, which is the one thing this gate cannot
# afford.
#
# SCOPE. Only the hashed trees are pinned. R sources are NOT listed: nothing
# committed pins a code_fingerprint (checked -- no test verifies one), so
# forcing eol there would be scope creep dressed up as a fix. Recorded hashes
# are unchanged; LF is what they were computed over.
data-raw/**/*.csv   text eol=lf
data/*.csv          text eol=lf
data/anchors/**     text eol=lf

# Binaries must never be normalised -- any rewrite corrupts them outright.
*.rds   binary
*.mdb   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.png   binary
*.pdf   binary
